import hashlib
import json

def sha256(text: str) -> str:
    return hashlib.sha256(text.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest()

day39 = {'day': 39, 'title': 'Responsive Search with useDeferredValue: Debouncing vs Deferred Rendering Benchmarks', 'desc': "Master React's useDeferredValue hook: explore the dual-render lifecycle, benchmark deferred rendering against fixed timer debouncing, implement visual staleness signaling (query !== deferredQuery), and combine deferred values with React.memo bailouts.", 'minutes': 38, 'parts': [{'title': 'The Dual-Render Lifecycle of useDeferredValue: Urgent First, Deferred Second', 'say': ["In Day 38, we mastered useTransition, which empowers developers to wrap state update dispatchers inside startTransition to mark them as low-priority. However, in real-world application architectures, components often receive state values indirectly as props from parent components or external consumer libraries. In these scenarios, the child component does not control the setState call; it simply receives a new raw value—such as an active search query string, a slider numerical value, or an active filter object. How can a child component defer its own expensive rendering when it cannot control the parent's state dispatch? React 18 provides the exact answer to this architectural challenge: useDeferredValue.", "To understand useDeferredValue, we must trace its internal dual-render lifecycle. When a parent component passes an updated value to a child (for example, the user types a new character so the query prop changes from 'Rea' to 'React'), useDeferredValue does not simply update immediately. Instead, React executes a two-phase rendering dance.", "In Phase 1 (the Urgent Pass), React renders the component immediately using the previous, stale value. That is, if the incoming prop is 'React', useDeferredValue('React') returns 'Rea'! Because it returns the old value, any heavy child subtree that is wrapped in React.memo will observe identical props ('Rea' === 'Rea') and bail out of rendering entirely! Consequently, the urgent render completes in less than 1 millisecond. The browser immediately updates the user's text cursor and displays the typed character without a microsecond of typing lag.", "Immediately after the urgent render commits to the screen, Phase 2 (the Deferred Pass) begins. React's scheduler automatically dispatches a background render in TransitionLane, this time passing the new value ('React') to useDeferredValue. The heavy child subtree reconciles concurrently in the background across cooperative 5ms macro-task slices. The moment the background computation finishes, React commits the updated filtered results to the screen.", 'Notice the profound elegance of this mechanism: without needing any wrapper functions, timers, or explicit transition callbacks, useDeferredValue bifurcates a single prop change into an instant urgent render followed by a time-sliced background render. The user gets instantaneous input responsiveness, and the application renders heavy computational trees with zero thread contention.', 'In addition, this two-phase lifecycle provides a natural way to communicate pending states to the user. By comparing the fresh prop directly against the deferred value (`query !== deferredQuery`), the component can immediately detect whether the displayed UI is currently stale. This enables sophisticated visual cues—such as dimming the results list or showing an inline spinner—without any additional boolean state flags.', 'In the executable simulation below, we model this dual-render lifecycle. Watch how the engine executes Phase 1 with the old value, bails out child re-renders, commits the urgent input instantly, and then executes Phase 2 in the background to commit the final data. Run the code to observe the two-phase lifecycle in action.', "To fully master `useDeferredValue`, we must examine how it interacts with the React Fiber reconciliation engine at the tree level. When a component calls `const deferred = useDeferredValue(value)`, React allocates an internal hook record in the Fiber's `memoizedState` linked list. During the initial urgent render pass, React inspects the current render lanes. Because the render was triggered by an urgent user interaction (such as an `onChange` event in `SyncLane`), React sets `deferred` to its previously committed value rather than the incoming value.", 'This behavior ensures that the immediate work-in-progress tree created during the urgent pass is mathematically identical to the existing current tree with respect to the deferred value. When child components evaluate `props.query === prevProps.query`, the reference comparison evaluates to true with zero CPU overhead. Only after the urgent commit completes and the browser paints the new text character onto the monitor does React trigger a second, separate render pass in `TransitionLane`. During this second pass, the hook record is updated to return the new value, allowing child components to re-render in the background without blocking the UI.'], 'code': '// The Dual-Render Lifecycle of useDeferredValue\nclass DeferredValueLifecycle {\n  constructor() {\n    this.currentValue = \'Rea\';\n    this.deferredValue = \'Rea\';\n    this.childRenderCount = 0;\n  }\n\n  // Simulating incoming prop update: \'Rea\' -> \'React\'\n  receiveNewProp(incomingProp) {\n    console.log(\'--- Incoming Prop Update: "\' + incomingProp + \'" ---\');\n    this.currentValue = incomingProp;\n\n    // Phase 1: Urgent Render Pass (SyncLane)\n    // useDeferredValue returns the PREVIOUS value (\'Rea\')\n    console.log(\'[Phase 1: Urgent Pass] Input updated to: "\' + this.currentValue + \'"\');\n    console.log(\'[Phase 1: Urgent Pass] useDeferredValue returns stale: "\' + this.deferredValue + \'"\');\n\n    // Child checks if deferred value changed (Memo Bailout!)\n    const didBailout = this.renderChild(this.deferredValue);\n    console.log(\'[Phase 1: Memo Check] Child subtree bailed out? \' + didBailout + \' (0ms wasted)\');\n\n    // Phase 2: Deferred Render Pass (TransitionLane)\n    // Scheduled in background macro-task with the NEW value (\'React\')\n    this.scheduleDeferredPass(incomingProp);\n  }\n\n  renderChild(val) {\n    // If val matches previously rendered value, bail out\n    if (val === this.lastRenderedVal) {\n      return true; // Bailed out!\n    }\n    this.childRenderCount++;\n    this.lastRenderedVal = val;\n    console.log(\'  -> [Heavy Render] Reconciled 10,000 items for query: "\' + val + \'"\');\n    return false;\n  }\n\n  scheduleDeferredPass(newVal) {\n    console.log(\'\\n[Phase 2: Deferred Pass] Background macro-task dispatched\');\n    this.deferredValue = newVal;\n    this.renderChild(this.deferredValue);\n    console.log(\'[Phase 2: Commit] Deferred results committed to DOM for: "\' + this.deferredValue + \'"\');\n  }\n}\n\nconst engine = new DeferredValueLifecycle();\nengine.lastRenderedVal = \'Rea\'; // Initial mount\n\n// User types \'ct\' to form \'React\'\nengine.receiveNewProp(\'React\');\nconsole.log(\'\\nTotal Heavy Child Renders: \' + engine.childRenderCount + \' (Only 1 render instead of 2!)\');\n', 'output': '--- Incoming Prop Update: "React" ---\n[Phase 1: Urgent Pass] Input updated to: "React"\n[Phase 1: Urgent Pass] useDeferredValue returns stale: "Rea"\n[Phase 1: Memo Check] Child subtree bailed out? true (0ms wasted)\n\n[Phase 2: Deferred Pass] Background macro-task dispatched\n  -> [Heavy Render] Reconciled 10,000 items for query: "React"\n[Phase 2: Commit] Deferred results committed to DOM for: "React"\n\nTotal Heavy Child Renders: 1 (Only 1 render instead of 2!)', 'codeNotes': [{'line': 2, 'note': 'DeferredValueLifecycle models the two-phase urgent and deferred rendering dance.'}, {'line': 13, 'note': "In Phase 1, useDeferredValue returns the stale value ('Rea') to allow memo bailout."}, {'line': 18, 'note': "Child component detects 'Rea' === 'Rea' and skips expensive reconciliation."}, {'line': 29, 'note': "Phase 2 runs in a background macro-task with 'React', updating the results smoothly."}], 'tryIt': "Simulate typing 'React 19' and trace how Phase 1 returns 'React' before Phase 2 renders 'React 19'.", 'check': {'question': 'What value does useDeferredValue(newVal) return during the initial urgent render pass when a new prop arrives?', 'options': ['It returns the previous (old) value, allowing memoized children to bail out of rendering while the input updates instantly', 'It returns null', 'It returns a rejected promise', 'It returns an empty string'], 'answer': 0, 'why': 'During the first urgent pass, useDeferredValue returns the previous value so that React.memo components bail out, keeping the urgent keystroke render instant.'}}, {'title': 'useDeferredValue vs setTimeout Debounce: Architectural Benchmarks', 'say': ['For over fifteen years, the standard frontend recipe for managing heavy search inputs was debouncing using setTimeout. When a user typed, an event handler would clear the existing timer and start a new 300ms timer. Only after the user stopped typing for 300 consecutive milliseconds would the heavy calculation finally execute. While debouncing was a valuable technique in the pre-concurrent era, comparing debouncing to useDeferredValue reveals that timer-based debouncing is an obsolete, blunt instrument that introduces severe UX flaws.', 'Let us examine the three fundamental flaws of setTimeout debouncing. First, debouncing is rigidly fixed to an arbitrary time duration (e.g. 300ms). On a high-end M3 Max MacBook Pro or high-performance desktop gaming rig with 16 fast CPU cores, the heavy filter calculation might only take 2 milliseconds to execute. Yet, a debounced input forces the user on high-end hardware to wait an artificial 300 milliseconds of dead idle time before search results appear! The user perceives the application as laggy and slow, even though the hardware was capable of instant rendering.', 'Second, debouncing fails catastrophically on slow devices. On a budget mobile Android smartphone where the filter calculation takes 400 milliseconds, a 300ms debounce timer simply delays the freeze. The user pauses typing for 300ms, the timer fires, and the heavy 400ms calculation immediately locks the main thread! If the user attempts to resume typing during that 400ms freeze, the phone stutters, drops frames, and misses touch events. Debouncing did not prevent the freeze; it merely postponed it.', "In contrast, useDeferredValue is completely adaptive and responsive to device capability. It does not use a timer. If the user is on a blazing-fast computer and the CPU is idle, the deferred render begins on the very next macro-task (less than 1ms delay) and commits results almost instantaneously. If the user is on a slow budget smartphone and types rapidly, useDeferredValue yields cooperatively across 5ms slices, automatically adapting its execution pace to match the hardware's exact computational capacity. It renders as fast as the hardware can go, without ever blocking user typing.", 'Furthermore, debouncing delays network requests and visual feedback uniformly regardless of context. If a user types a single character and pauses, debouncing still forces a 300ms wait. With useDeferredValue, rendering begins immediately without artificial delays. If a second character arrives while the first is rendering, the first render is aborted cleanly via preemption.', 'Study the benchmark simulation below. We compare fixed 300ms debouncing against adaptive useDeferredValue under fast and slow device conditions. Notice how useDeferredValue delivers 30x faster results on capable hardware and prevents thread freezes on constrained hardware. Run the code to observe the benchmark results.', "A deep architectural analysis of timer-based debouncing versus deferred rendering highlights the fundamental philosophy of React's concurrent design: declarative scheduling versus imperative timing. When developers use `setTimeout` or `lodash.debounce`, they are attempting to guess the speed of the user's hardware, the complexity of the dataset, and the current load on the CPU. A fixed 300ms timer is an arbitrary compromise that is simultaneously too slow for fast devices and too fast for slow devices.", "By contrast, `useDeferredValue` does not guess or enforce arbitrary sleep delays. It operates on an adaptive, capacity-driven feedback loop with the browser event loop. If the user's device has surplus CPU headroom, the background render completes in 5 milliseconds, giving the user near-instantaneous search results. If the device is constrained or if the user is typing rapidly, React automatically yields control, segments rendering into 5ms slices, and drops obsolete intermediate renders. This means your application automatically delivers the fastest possible performance mathematically achievable on whatever device the customer happens to be using."], 'code': "// Benchmark: Fixed 300ms Debounce vs Adaptive useDeferredValue\nfunction benchmarkStrategies(deviceType, computeDurationMs) {\n  console.log('=== Benchmarking Strategy on: ' + deviceType + ' (Compute: ' + computeDurationMs + 'ms) ===');\n\n  // Strategy 1: Fixed 300ms Timer Debounce\n  const debounceDelayMs = 300;\n  const debounceTotalLatency = debounceDelayMs + computeDurationMs;\n  const debounceJank = computeDurationMs > 16.66 ? 'YES (FROZE UI FOR ' + computeDurationMs + 'ms!)' : 'NO';\n  console.log('[Debounce 300ms] Idle Wait: ' + debounceDelayMs + 'ms | Total Latency: ' + debounceTotalLatency + 'ms | Thread Freeze: ' + debounceJank);\n\n  // Strategy 2: Adaptive useDeferredValue (React Concurrent)\n  const deferredWaitMs = 1; // Dispatched immediately on next macro-task\n  const deferredTotalLatency = deferredWaitMs + computeDurationMs;\n  // Sliced into 5ms chunks -> 0ms thread freeze!\n  const deferredJank = 'NO (Sliced into 5ms chunks, 0 dropped frames)';\n  console.log('[useDeferredValue] Idle Wait: ' + deferredWaitMs + 'ms | Total Latency: ' + deferredTotalLatency + 'ms | Thread Freeze: ' + deferredJank);\n\n  const speedup = (debounceTotalLatency / deferredTotalLatency).toFixed(1);\n  console.log('[Verdict] useDeferredValue was ' + speedup + 'x faster and completely eliminated thread freezing!');\n}\n\n// Scenario A: Fast Modern Laptop (Compute takes only 8ms)\nbenchmarkStrategies('High-End Desktop (8ms compute)', 8);\nconsole.log('');\n// Scenario B: Low-End Budget Mobile (Compute takes 80ms)\nbenchmarkStrategies('Budget Smartphone (80ms compute)', 80);\n", 'output': '=== Benchmarking Strategy on: High-End Desktop (8ms compute) (Compute: 8ms) ===\n[Debounce 300ms] Idle Wait: 300ms | Total Latency: 308ms | Thread Freeze: NO\n[useDeferredValue] Idle Wait: 1ms | Total Latency: 9ms | Thread Freeze: NO (Sliced into 5ms chunks, 0 dropped frames)\n[Verdict] useDeferredValue was 34.2x faster and completely eliminated thread freezing!\n\n=== Benchmarking Strategy on: Budget Smartphone (80ms compute) (Compute: 80ms) ===\n[Debounce 300ms] Idle Wait: 300ms | Total Latency: 380ms | Thread Freeze: YES (FROZE UI FOR 80ms!)\n[useDeferredValue] Idle Wait: 1ms | Total Latency: 81ms | Thread Freeze: NO (Sliced into 5ms chunks, 0 dropped frames)\n[Verdict] useDeferredValue was 4.7x faster and completely eliminated thread freezing!', 'codeNotes': [{'line': 2, 'note': 'benchmarkStrategies compares fixed timer delay vs adaptive concurrent scheduling.'}, {'line': 7, 'note': 'Fixed debounce forces 300ms of artificial idle delay even when compute takes 8ms.'}, {'line': 13, 'note': 'useDeferredValue starts immediately on the next macro-task, adapting to device speed.'}, {'line': 24, 'note': 'On low-end hardware, 80ms compute is cooperatively sliced without freezing the phone.'}], 'tryIt': 'Test with a 2ms compute duration to see how useDeferredValue delivers results over 100x faster than debouncing.', 'check': {'question': 'Why is useDeferredValue superior to a fixed 300ms setTimeout debounce for responsive search inputs?', 'options': ['useDeferredValue adapts dynamically to device speed, rendering immediately if the CPU is free and time-slicing without freezing on slow devices', 'useDeferredValue runs on the GPU instead of the CPU', 'useDeferredValue automatically translates English queries into multiple languages', 'useDeferredValue deletes all components that take longer than 10 milliseconds'], 'answer': 0, 'why': 'useDeferredValue does not enforce an arbitrary fixed delay; it starts rendering immediately on available frame headroom and cooperatively slices execution to prevent UI freezes.'}}, {'title': 'Visual Staleness and Intentional Lag: query !== deferredQuery Styling', 'say': ["When implementing useDeferredValue, frontend engineers often ask: 'If useDeferredValue intentionally keeps showing the old value while the new value is rendering in the background, won't the user think the application is ignoring their input?' This is a perceptive question. If the user types 'Shoes' into a search box, the search input immediately displays 'Shoes', but the results grid beneath it continues displaying the results for 'Shoe' for 40 milliseconds until the background pass finishes. Without proper visual feedback, this discrepancy can cause momentary confusion.", 'To eliminate user confusion, React provides a built-in idiom for detecting and communicating visual staleness: comparing the raw prop directly against the deferred value: `const isStale = query !== deferredQuery;`. Because query updates in Phase 1 (urgent) while deferredQuery updates in Phase 2 (deferred), the condition `query !== deferredQuery` evaluates to true precisely during the background rendering window!', "This allows developers to apply intentional, elegant visual staleness styling. For example, developers can apply CSS styling such as `opacity: isStale ? 0.7 : 1.0`, `transition: 'opacity 0.2s ease'`, or a subtle CSS grayscale filter (`filter: isStale ? 'grayscale(40%)' : 'none'`). When the user types, the results grid dims slightly, indicating to the user: 'The results you see below correspond to your previous query while new results are being calculated.' The moment the deferred render commits, `query === deferredQuery` becomes true again, and the results grid smoothly snaps back to full brightness.", "This intentional lag pattern is vastly superior to showing a loading spinner. A loading spinner destroys the user's reading flow, hides the existing items, and causes layout jitter. Dimming the stale results maintains full reading context: the user can continue reading the old results, verify whether their desired product is already listed, and observe the new results materialize smoothly without any visual jarring.", 'In addition, intentional staleness can be combined with assistive technology tags. By adding `aria-busy={isStale}` to the results container, screen readers are informed that content is refreshing, providing an accessible experience for all users.', 'Study the visual staleness engine below. We define a StalenessIndicator class that monitors query updates, computes the staleness boolean (`query !== deferredQuery`), and dynamically applies opacity and transition filters. Run the code to trace how visual staleness provides feedback during background rendering.', "The visual staleness pattern (`query !== deferredQuery`) is a cornerstone of modern accessibility and perceptual psychology. Research in human-computer interaction demonstrates that when users initiate an action, their primary cognitive concern is confirmation of intent: 'Did the application register what I just typed?' Once intent is confirmed by seeing the typed character in the search box, the user shifts into an evaluative reading state.", "By reducing the opacity of stale results to 0.6 or 0.7 while the background pass computes, the application satisfies both cognitive needs simultaneously. The text box confirms immediate input capture, while the dimmed container clearly signals that a refresh is underway. Furthermore, because the previous items remain visible on the screen, users can continue skimming or even click on an existing result if it already matches their needs. This completely eliminates the frustrating 'layout shifts' and 'bouncing content' that plague applications relying on heavy full-container loading spinners.", "To make intentional staleness visually compelling, CSS transitions should be tuned specifically for concurrent rendering. Applying `transition: opacity 200ms ease-out` allows the visual dimming to appear subtle rather than flickering during rapid keystrokes. If the user pauses typing for even 50 milliseconds, the deferred pass completes, and the container fades smoothly back to full opacity. This creates a tactile, physical sensation of 'settling' that communicates high software craftsmanship and reassuring predictability to the end user."], 'code': '// Visual Staleness & Intentional Lag Engine (query !== deferredQuery)\nclass StalenessVisualEngine {\n  constructor() {\n    this.rawQuery = \'Apple\';\n    this.deferredQuery = \'Apple\';\n  }\n\n  // User types \'s\' -> rawQuery updates immediately\n  onUserKeystroke(newText) {\n    this.rawQuery = newText;\n    console.log(\'[Keystroke] Raw query updated to: "\' + this.rawQuery + \'"\');\n\n    // Audit staleness immediately in Phase 1\n    const visualState = this.getVisualState();\n    console.log(\'[Phase 1: Urgent Pass] Staleness: \' + visualState.isStale + \' | Opacity: \' + visualState.opacity + \' | Label: \' + visualState.statusLabel);\n    console.log(\'  -> Displayed Results: [Showing stale matches for "\' + this.deferredQuery + \'"]\');\n\n    // Simulate Phase 2 background deferred resolution\n    this.resolveDeferredPass(newText);\n  }\n\n  resolveDeferredPass(newVal) {\n    this.deferredQuery = newVal;\n    const visualState = this.getVisualState();\n    console.log(\'\\n[Phase 2: Deferred Pass] Deferred query updated to: "\' + this.deferredQuery + \'"\');\n    console.log(\'[Phase 2: Commit] Staleness: \' + visualState.isStale + \' | Opacity: \' + visualState.opacity + \' | Label: \' + visualState.statusLabel);\n    console.log(\'  -> Displayed Results: [Showing fresh matches for "\' + this.deferredQuery + \'"]\');\n  }\n\n  getVisualState() {\n    const isStale = this.rawQuery !== this.deferredQuery;\n    return {\n      isStale: isStale,\n      opacity: isStale ? 0.6 : 1.0,\n      filter: isStale ? \'grayscale(30%)\' : \'none\',\n      statusLabel: isStale ? \'Updating results...\' : \'Synced\'\n    };\n  }\n}\n\nconst ui = new StalenessVisualEngine();\nconsole.log(\'--- Initial Synced State ---\');\nconsole.log(\'Raw: "\' + ui.rawQuery + \'" | Deferred: "\' + ui.deferredQuery + \'" | Status: \' + ui.getVisualState().statusLabel);\n\nconsole.log(\'\\n--- User Types: "Apples" ---\');\nui.onUserKeystroke(\'Apples\');\n', 'output': '--- Initial Synced State ---\nRaw: "Apple" | Deferred: "Apple" | Status: Synced\n\n--- User Types: "Apples" ---\n[Keystroke] Raw query updated to: "Apples"\n[Phase 1: Urgent Pass] Staleness: true | Opacity: 0.6 | Label: Updating results...\n  -> Displayed Results: [Showing stale matches for "Apple"]\n\n[Phase 2: Deferred Pass] Deferred query updated to: "Apples"\n[Phase 2: Commit] Staleness: false | Opacity: 1 | Label: Synced\n  -> Displayed Results: [Showing fresh matches for "Apples"]', 'codeNotes': [{'line': 2, 'note': 'StalenessVisualEngine computes UI visual state by comparing rawQuery with deferredQuery.'}, {'line': 10, 'note': 'isStale evaluates to true immediately in Phase 1, dimming the results container.'}, {'line': 28, 'note': 'getVisualState encapsulates opacity, CSS grayscale filters, and status labels.'}, {'line': 39, 'note': 'Notice how the UI smoothly transitions from Synced -> Updating results... -> Synced.'}], 'tryIt': 'Add a CSS transition property to getVisualState and verify that visual styling adapts cleanly.', 'check': {'question': 'How do React developers detect that a component is currently rendering a deferred background update when using useDeferredValue?', 'options': ['By comparing the raw prop directly against the deferred value: `const isStale = query !== deferredQuery;`', 'By inspecting the window.navigator object', 'By throwing an error inside the component body', "By checking if localStorage contains the key '__IS_DEFERRED__'"], 'answer': 0, 'why': 'During the background deferred pass, `query` reflects the new value while `deferredQuery` still reflects the old value, making `query !== deferredQuery` the official staleness indicator.'}}, {'title': 'Bailout Optimization with React.memo: Preventing Premature Child Renders', 'say': ['We now arrive at a critical architectural requirement that many intermediate React developers miss: useDeferredValue is ineffective unless the child component receiving the deferred value is wrapped in React.memo! To understand why React.memo is mandatory, we must revisit the fundamental rendering rules of React.', "In standard React component execution, whenever a parent component re-renders, React unconditionally re-renders all child components nested within that parent's JSX tree, regardless of whether the children's props changed! Now consider what happens if you call useDeferredValue in a parent component without memoizing the heavy child list: When the user types, the parent component re-renders in Phase 1 (the urgent pass). If `<HeavyList query={deferredQuery} />` is not wrapped in React.memo, React will immediately execute `<HeavyList>` synchronously during Phase 1!", "Even though `deferredQuery` still holds the old value ('Rea'), `<HeavyList>` will re-run its entire render function, calculating virtual DOM nodes and running layout algorithms synchronously during the urgent pass! The entire benefit of useDeferredValue is destroyed: the main thread freezes, keystrokes drop frames, and the application stutters. All because the developer forgot to memoize the child component.", "When you wrap the child component in React.memo—`const MemoizedHeavyList = React.memo(HeavyList);`—the behavior transforms completely. During Phase 1, React checks whether the props passed to `MemoizedHeavyList` have changed. Because `deferredQuery` still holds the previous value ('Rea' === 'Rea'), React.memo returns true, and React bails out of rendering `MemoizedHeavyList` entirely! Phase 1 completes in less than a millisecond, leaving the thread 100% free for the browser to paint the urgent keystroke.", "Only in Phase 2, when React dispatches the deferred macro-task with the new value ('React'), does `MemoizedHeavyList` see a new prop ('Rea' !== 'React'). It reconciles concurrently in the background, time-sliced across 5ms MessageChannel slices. Wrapping child components in React.memo is therefore not an optional micro-optimization; it is an architectural prerequisite for useDeferredValue.", 'Examine the memo bailout simulator below. We compare the render counts of an unmemoized child versus a memoized child during Phase 1 and Phase 2. Observe how React.memo bails out completely during the urgent pass. Run the code to verify the bailout behavior.', "To understand why `React.memo` is so fundamentally intertwined with `useDeferredValue`, we must inspect the Fiber work loop's bailout algorithm: `checkScheduledUpdateOrNewPropsIfWorkInProgress`. When React visits a Fiber node during the work loop, it evaluates whether that Fiber has pending work scheduled in the active render lane. If the component is a standard, unmemoized function component, React unconditionally proceeds to execute the function body, assuming that the parent re-render might have generated new JSX elements.", 'However, when the component is wrapped in `React.memo`, React invokes its shallow equality comparison (`shallowEqual(prevProps, nextProps)`). Because `useDeferredValue` returned the old value during Phase 1, `nextProps` and `prevProps` are completely identical. React immediately executes a bailout: it clones the existing Fiber without executing the component function, skips the entire child subtree, and moves straight to the next sibling! This bailout reduces what would have been a 50-millisecond component reconciliation into a sub-microsecond pointer swap, preserving the frame budget entirely.', 'Beyond simple prop comparisons, React developers can also use the `useMemo` hook inside the parent component to cache expensive subtrees when `useDeferredValue` is employed. For example, `const memoizedList = useMemo(() => <HeavyList query={deferredQuery} />, [deferredQuery]);`. Because `deferredQuery` does not change during Phase 1, the `useMemo` dependency array evaluates to unchanged, and React reuses the exact same JSX element reference, triggering the identical bailout optimization without needing an explicit `React.memo` wrapper on the child component.', "This bailout behavior underscores a key architectural rule: in React's concurrent model, performance optimization is achieved through structural design rather than manual code obfuscation. By pairing declarative hooks like `useDeferredValue` with memoized component boundaries, developers allow the engine to intelligently prune entire subtrees from execution without writing fragile imperative caching logic."], 'code': '// The React.memo Bailout Prerequisite for useDeferredValue\nclass MemoBailoutSimulator {\n  constructor() {\n    this.unmemoizedRenderCount = 0;\n    this.memoizedRenderCount = 0;\n    this.prevMemoizedProp = null;\n  }\n\n  // Simulating Phase 1 (Urgent Pass) with stale deferred value: \'A\'\n  simulatePhase1(staleDeferredVal) {\n    console.log(\'--- Phase 1: Urgent Input Pass (DeferredVal = "\' + staleDeferredVal + \'") ---\');\n\n    // 1. Unmemoized child ALWAYS executes on parent render!\n    this.unmemoizedRenderCount++;\n    console.log(\'[Unmemoized Child] Re-rendered unnecessarily in Phase 1! Count=\' + this.unmemoizedRenderCount);\n\n    // 2. Memoized child checks props equality (Bailout Check)\n    if (staleDeferredVal === this.prevMemoizedProp) {\n      console.log(\'[React.memo Child] BAILOUT! Props identical ("\' + staleDeferredVal + \'" === "\' + this.prevMemoizedProp + \'"). Skipped render!\');\n    } else {\n      this.memoizedRenderCount++;\n      this.prevMemoizedProp = staleDeferredVal;\n      console.log(\'[React.memo Child] Re-rendered. Count=\' + this.memoizedRenderCount);\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Simulating Phase 2 (Deferred Pass) with fresh deferred value: \'AB\'\n  simulatePhase2(newDeferredVal) {\n    console.log(\'\\n--- Phase 2: Deferred Pass (DeferredVal = "\' + newDeferredVal + \'") ---\');\n\n    // Both children render the new value\n    this.unmemoizedRenderCount++;\n    console.log(\'[Unmemoized Child] Re-rendered Phase 2. Count=\' + this.unmemoizedRenderCount);\n\n    if (newDeferredVal !== this.prevMemoizedProp) {\n      this.memoizedRenderCount++;\n      this.prevMemoizedProp = newDeferredVal;\n      console.log(\'[React.memo Child] Re-rendered Phase 2 concurrently. Count=\' + this.memoizedRenderCount);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nconst sim = new MemoBailoutSimulator();\nsim.prevMemoizedProp = \'A\'; // Initially mounted with \'A\'\n\n// User types \'B\'\nsim.simulatePhase1(\'A\');  // Phase 1 with old value \'A\'\nsim.simulatePhase2(\'AB\'); // Phase 2 with new value \'AB\'\n\nconsole.log(\'\\nFinal Comparison:\');\nconsole.log(\'Unmemoized Child Renders: \' + sim.unmemoizedRenderCount + \' (Wasted 1 render in urgent pass!)\');\nconsole.log(\'React.memo Child Renders: \' + sim.memoizedRenderCount + \' (Optimal: only rendered once!)\');\n', 'output': '--- Phase 1: Urgent Input Pass (DeferredVal = "A") ---\n[Unmemoized Child] Re-rendered unnecessarily in Phase 1! Count=1\n[React.memo Child] BAILOUT! Props identical ("A" === "A"). Skipped render!\n\n--- Phase 2: Deferred Pass (DeferredVal = "AB") ---\n[Unmemoized Child] Re-rendered Phase 2. Count=2\n[React.memo Child] Re-rendered Phase 2 concurrently. Count=1\n\nFinal Comparison:\nUnmemoized Child Renders: 2 (Wasted 1 render in urgent pass!)\nReact.memo Child Renders: 1 (Optimal: only rendered once!)', 'codeNotes': [{'line': 2, 'note': 'MemoBailoutSimulator contrasts unmemoized vs memoized child component execution.'}, {'line': 13, 'note': 'Without React.memo, children re-render unconditionally in Phase 1, freezing the UI.'}, {'line': 17, 'note': 'With React.memo, identical props trigger an instant bailout, saving full reconciliation.'}, {'line': 39, 'note': 'React.memo cuts total renders in half, ensuring Phase 1 completes under 1ms.'}], 'tryIt': "Add a third prop update 'ABC' and verify that React.memo continues bailing out during Phase 1.", 'check': {'question': 'Why is wrapping the heavy child component in React.memo mandatory when using useDeferredValue?', 'options': ['Because without React.memo, the child will re-render unconditionally during the initial urgent pass, destroying the performance benefit of useDeferredValue', 'Because React.memo is required to enable CSS stylesheets', 'Because useDeferredValue throws a runtime error if React.memo is missing', 'Because browsers refuse to render HTML unless React.memo is declared'], 'answer': 0, 'why': 'Without React.memo, the child component re-renders during the urgent pass regardless of whether the deferred value changed, freezing the main thread.'}}, {'title': 'Building a Production Responsive Search Engine with useDeferredValue in Pure JavaScript', 'say': ['In this final section of Day 39, we synthesize all concepts learned today into an enterprise-grade Responsive Search Engine implemented in pure JavaScript. Our engine incorporates the two-phase urgent and deferred rendering lifecycle, React.memo bailout verification, visual staleness styling calculation (`query !== deferredQuery`), and benchmark telemetry tracking.', 'Engineering this search engine requires structuring three decoupled modules. First, the SearchInputController manages the raw input state and captures user typing events. Second, the DeferredValueManager coordinates the two-phase lifecycle, returning the previous value during Phase 1 to trigger memo bailouts and dispatching the background macro-task for Phase 2. Third, the MemoizedResultsGrid performs filtering over a 10,000-record dataset, reporting execution timing and bailout statistics.', 'Notice how cleanly this architecture mirrors production React 18 applications. When a user types into our engine, the text input updates instantaneously, the results grid dims slightly to communicate intentional staleness, and the background deferred pass computes the filtered dataset without dropping a single frame.', 'By mastering the nuances of useDeferredValue and combining it with React.memo bailouts and visual staleness cues, you possess the architectural mastery required to build lightning-fast, high-throughput search interfaces that scale to massive enterprise datasets.', 'Review the complete, fully functioning Responsive Search Engine below. We simulate a user typing consecutive characters into the search bar, verify memo bailouts during Phase 1, observe Phase 2 background commits, and review comprehensive performance telemetry. Run the code to verify that our engine operates with 100% precision.', 'In production enterprise systems, combining `useDeferredValue` with virtualization libraries (such as `@tanstack/react-virtual` or `react-window`) produces extraordinary performance synergies. Virtualization ensures that only the visible rows in the viewport are mounted into the DOM, while `useDeferredValue` ensures that filtering or sorting those rows never blocks user input. Together, these two techniques allow React web applications to handle datasets of 100,000+ records with native 60 FPS fluidity.', "When profiling your application in Chrome DevTools or React Developer Tools, look for the 'Deferred Value' tag in the Profiler flamegraph. You will see a microscopic commit in `SyncLane` containing only the input element update, followed by a wider, cooperatively sliced commit in `TransitionLane` containing the virtual grid render. This visual signature confirms that your component architecture is fully optimized for concurrent rendering.", 'When deploying `useDeferredValue` across enterprise applications with internationalization, complex regex search filters, or fuzzy-matching algorithms (such as Fuse.js or Levenshtein distance calculations), understanding the computational budget of each slice is essential. Heavy string comparison algorithms can quickly consume 50 to 100 milliseconds when executed against tens of thousands of items. By combining `useDeferredValue` with cooperative generator loops or Web Workers, engineers ensure that even the most demanding algorithmic searches run seamlessly without causing dropped frames on budget hardware.', 'Finally, consider the holistic design of modern React concurrent applications. In Day 36, we built the 16.6ms frame budget and MessageChannel time-slicing foundation. In Day 37, we explored 31-bit Lane bitmasks and priority starvation escalation. In Day 38, we mastered `useTransition` and `isPending`. And today, in Day 39, we conquered `useDeferredValue`, React.memo bailouts, and visual staleness cues. Tomorrow, on Day 40, we synthesize every single one of these cutting-edge techniques into our ultimate Milestone 7 capstone: the Low-Latency 10,000-Node Visual Graph Filter.', 'As you integrate these patterns into your daily development workflow, you will discover that concurrent rendering transforms how you approach application architecture. Instead of treating performance as an afterthought or adding clumsy loading states, you design interfaces that naturally adapt to device speeds, gracefully handle heavy computational loads, and maintain an unwavering standard of 60 FPS responsiveness across any user interaction.'], 'code': '// Production Responsive Search Engine with useDeferredValue\nclass ProductionSearchEngine {\n  constructor(dataset) {\n    this.dataset = dataset;\n    this.rawQuery = \'\';\n    this.deferredQuery = \'\';\n    this.lastRenderedQuery = \'\';\n    this.telemetry = {\n      urgentKeystrokes: 0,\n      memoBailouts: 0,\n      deferredRenders: 0,\n      matchesCount: 0\n    };\n  }\n\n  // User types character -> Phase 1 (Urgent Pass)\n  handleKeystroke(char) {\n    this.rawQuery += char;\n    this.telemetry.urgentKeystrokes++;\n    console.log(\'[Urgent Pass] Keystroke registered: "\' + this.rawQuery + \'" (Instant)\');\n\n    // Check memo bailout: child sees this.deferredQuery (still holds old value!)\n    const isStale = this.rawQuery !== this.deferredQuery;\n    console.log(\'  -> Visual State: Stale=\' + isStale + \' | Opacity=\' + (isStale ? 0.6 : 1.0));\n\n    if (this.deferredQuery === this.lastRenderedQuery) {\n      this.telemetry.memoBailouts++;\n      console.log(\'  -> [Memo Bailout] Child skipped rendering! Showing cached results for: "\' + this.lastRenderedQuery + \'"\');\n    }\n\n    // Trigger Phase 2 (Deferred Pass)\n    this.flushDeferredPass();\n  }\n\n  // Phase 2: Background Deferred Pass\n  flushDeferredPass() {\n    this.deferredQuery = this.rawQuery;\n    console.log(\'\\n[Deferred Pass] Background filter running for: "\' + this.deferredQuery + \'"\');\n\n    // Filter dataset\n    const q = this.deferredQuery.toLowerCase();\n    const results = this.dataset.filter(function(item) {\n      return item.name.toLowerCase().indexOf(q) !== -1;\n    });\n\n    this.lastRenderedQuery = this.deferredQuery;\n    this.telemetry.deferredRenders++;\n    this.telemetry.matchesCount = results.length;\n\n    console.log(\'[Deferred Commit] Reconciled \' + results.length + \' matches | Opacity=1.0 | Status=Synced\');\n  }\n}\n\n// 10 sample products representing enterprise catalog\nconst sampleCatalog = [\n  { id: 1, name: \'React Native Mobile\' },\n  { id: 2, name: \'React Server Components\' },\n  { id: 3, name: \'Redux Toolkit Store\' },\n  { id: 4, name: \'Reactive Streams Engine\' },\n  { id: 5, name: \'TypeScript Compiler\' }\n];\n\nconst engine = new ProductionSearchEngine(sampleCatalog);\n\nconsole.log(\'--- Step 1: User types "R" ---\');\nengine.handleKeystroke(\'R\');\n\nconsole.log(\'\\n--- Step 2: User types "e" (query becomes "Re") ---\');\nengine.handleKeystroke(\'e\');\n\nconsole.log(\'\\n--- Final Search Engine Telemetry ---\');\nconsole.log(\'Total Keystrokes: \' + engine.telemetry.urgentKeystrokes);\nconsole.log(\'Memo Bailouts during Urgent Passes: \' + engine.telemetry.memoBailouts);\nconsole.log(\'Deferred Background Renders: \' + engine.telemetry.deferredRenders);\nconsole.log(\'Final Matches Count: \' + engine.telemetry.matchesCount);\n', 'output': '--- Step 1: User types "R" ---\n[Urgent Pass] Keystroke registered: "R" (Instant)\n  -> Visual State: Stale=true | Opacity=0.6\n  -> [Memo Bailout] Child skipped rendering! Showing cached results for: ""\n\n[Deferred Pass] Background filter running for: "R"\n[Deferred Commit] Reconciled 5 matches | Opacity=1.0 | Status=Synced\n\n--- Step 2: User types "e" (query becomes "Re") ---\n[Urgent Pass] Keystroke registered: "Re" (Instant)\n  -> Visual State: Stale=true | Opacity=0.6\n  -> [Memo Bailout] Child skipped rendering! Showing cached results for: "R"\n\n[Deferred Pass] Background filter running for: "Re"\n[Deferred Commit] Reconciled 4 matches | Opacity=1.0 | Status=Synced\n\n--- Final Search Engine Telemetry ---\nTotal Keystrokes: 2\nMemo Bailouts during Urgent Passes: 2\nDeferred Background Renders: 2\nFinal Matches Count: 4', 'codeNotes': [{'line': 2, 'note': 'ProductionSearchEngine encapsulates two-phase deferred rendering with memo bailouts.'}, {'line': 15, 'note': 'handleKeystroke executes Phase 1 immediately in SyncLane with intentional dimming.'}, {'line': 21, 'note': 'Memo bailout skips heavy filtering during the urgent pass, preserving typing fluidity.'}, {'line': 31, 'note': 'flushDeferredPass executes the heavy filter in the background and commits results.'}], 'tryIt': "Add a third keystroke 'a' ('Rea') and observe how memo bailouts scale across continuous typing.", 'check': {'question': 'What sequence of events occurs when a user types a new letter into an input using useDeferredValue and React.memo?', 'options': ['Phase 1 renders the urgent input with the old deferred value (child bails out via React.memo), then Phase 2 renders the heavy child in the background with the new value', 'The browser reloads the webpage from scratch', 'All React components are unmounted permanently', 'The operating system terminates the Node.js process'], 'answer': 0, 'why': 'Phase 1 updates the input immediately with the previous value (triggering a React.memo bailout in the child), and Phase 2 renders the heavy child concurrently in the background.'}}], 'summary': ['useDeferredValue bifurcates prop updates into a two-phase lifecycle: an urgent pass with the old value, followed by a deferred background pass with the new value.', 'Unlike fixed 300ms setTimeout debouncing, useDeferredValue adapts dynamically to device CPU capacity, rendering instantly if the CPU is free and time-slicing on slow hardware.', 'Comparing raw props against deferred values (query !== deferredQuery) enables intentional visual staleness styling (e.g. opacity: 0.6) without layout-shifting spinners.', 'Wrapping child components in React.memo is mandatory; without memoization, children re-render unconditionally during the urgent pass, destroying performance.', 'Production search engines combine useDeferredValue, React.memo bailouts, and staleness indicators to achieve 60 FPS typing responsiveness over 10,000+ item catalogs.'], 'projectStep': {'title': 'useDeferredValue Responsive Search Engine', 'steps': ['Identify search inputs where heavy filtering causes keystroke lag or dropped animation frames.', 'Pass the raw search query through useDeferredValue to decouple urgent typing from heavy filtering.', 'Wrap the heavy list or grid component in React.memo to guarantee bailouts during the initial urgent pass.', 'Apply intentional visual staleness styles (opacity: 0.6) whenever query !== deferredQuery.', 'Benchmark in Chrome DevTools to verify that input latency remains under 16ms and Total Blocking Time is zero.']}}
